WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.930
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:09.349
Today we are going to cover many important
questions in life.

00:00:09.349 --> 00:00:12.409
For instance, who is this?

00:00:12.409 --> 00:00:14.000
This is Halle Berry.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.660
Now, I show you this, who is it?

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:20.849
It is, of course, also Halle Berry.

00:00:20.849 --> 00:00:24.950
And if I show you this piece of text, who
does it refer to?

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.250
Again, Halle Berry.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.679
So why are these questions interesting?

00:00:29.679 --> 00:00:36.100
Well, an earlier paper found out from brain
readings that we indeed have person neurons

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.510
in our brain.

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:42.540
These are neurons specialized to recognize
a particular human being.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.510
That is quite interesting.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:48.960
And what is even more interesting is that
not that we have person neurons, but that

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.540
these neurons are multimodal.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:52.600
What does that mean?

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.820
This means that we understand the essence
of what makes Halle Berry, regardless of whether

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:01.460
it is a photo, a drawing, or anything else.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:02.700
I see, alright.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:10.440
Well then, our first question today is, do
neural networks also have multimodal neurons?

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:11.440
Not necessarily.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.990
The human brain is an inspiration for an artificial
neural network that we can simulate on our

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:20.380
computers, but do they work like the brain?

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:26.660
Well, if we study their inner workings, our
likely answer will be no, not in the slightest.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:31.340
But still, no one can stop us from a little
experimentation, so let’s try this for a

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:34.150
common neural network architecture.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:40.200
This neuron responds to human faces and says
that this is indeed a human face.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.800
So far so good.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:47.010
Now, if we provide it with a drawing of a
human face, it won’t recognize it to be

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:48.110
a face.

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:54.409
Well, so much for this multimodal idea, this
one is surely not a brain in a jar.

00:01:54.409 --> 00:01:58.210
But wait, we don’t give up so easily around
here.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:03.770
This is not the only neural network architecture
that exists, let’s grab a different one.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:09.929
This one is called OpenAI’s CLIP, and it
is remarkably good at generalizing concepts.

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:14.970
Let’s see how it can deal with the same
problem.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:18.450
Yes, this neuron responds to spiders, and
Spiderman.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.400
That’s the easy part.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:26.069
Now, please hold on to your papers, because
now comes the hard part.

00:02:26.069 --> 00:02:29.830
Drawings and comics of spiders and Spiderman.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:33.650
Yes, it responds to that too!

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:34.750
Wonderful.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:39.540
Now comes the final boss, which is spider-related
writings.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.920
And.. it responds to that too.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.580
Now, of course, this doesn’t mean that this
neural network would be a brain in a jar,

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:53.090
but it is a tiny bit closer to our thinking
than previous architectures.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:59.239
And now comes the best part, this insight
opens up the possibility for three amazing

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:00.590
experiments.

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.139
Experiment number one.

00:03:02.139 --> 00:03:03.139
Essence.

00:03:03.139 --> 00:03:08.260
So it appears to understand the essence of
a concept or a person.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:14.029
That is absolutely amazing, so I wonder if
we can turn this problem around and ask what

00:03:14.029 --> 00:03:16.909
it thinks about different concepts?

00:03:16.909 --> 00:03:22.120
It would be the equivalent to saying give
me all things spiders and Spiderman.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.309
Let’s do that with Lady Gaga.

00:03:25.309 --> 00:03:28.959
It says this is the essence of Lady Gaga.

00:03:28.959 --> 00:03:31.930
We get the smug smile, very good.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:37.639
And it says that the essence of Jesus Christ
is this, and it also includes the crown of

00:03:37.639 --> 00:03:39.090
thorns.

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:40.989
So far, flying colors.

00:03:40.989 --> 00:03:47.450
Now, we will see some images of feelings,
some of you might find some of them disturbing.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:52.870
I think the vast majority of you humans will
be just fine looking at them, but I wanted

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:54.699
to let you know just in case.

00:03:54.699 --> 00:03:58.639
So, what is the essence of someone being shocked?

00:03:58.639 --> 00:04:00.379
Well, this.

00:04:00.379 --> 00:04:05.949
I can attest to that, this is basically me
when reading this paper.

00:04:05.949 --> 00:04:09.299
My eyes were popping out just like this.

00:04:09.299 --> 00:04:10.299
Sleepiness.

00:04:10.299 --> 00:04:13.879
Yes, that is a coffee person before coffee
alright.

00:04:13.879 --> 00:04:20.739
The happiness, crying and seriousness neurons
also embody these feelings really well.

00:04:20.739 --> 00:04:23.870
Experiment number two, adversarial attacks.

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:29.830
We know that OpenAI’s clip responds to photos
and drawings of the same thing, so let’s

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:34.240
try some nasty attacks involving combining
the two.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:38.400
When we give it these images, it can classify
them with ease.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.090
This is an Apple, this is a laptop, a mug,
and so on.

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:45.180
Nothing too crazy going on here.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:50.449
However, now, let’s prepare a nasty adversarial
attack.

00:04:50.449 --> 00:04:55.710
Previously, sophisticated techniques were
developed to fool a neural network by adding

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:58.780
some nearly imperceptible noise to an image.

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:01.360
Let’s have a look at how this works.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:07.560
First, we present a previous neural network
with an image of a bus, and it will successfully

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:11.300
tell us that yes, this is indeed a bus.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:12.530
Of course it does.

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:19.789
Now, we show it not an image of a bus, but
a bus plus some carefully crafted noise that

00:05:19.789 --> 00:05:26.360
is barely perceptible, that forces the neural
network to misclassify it as an ostrich.

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:31.270
I will stress that this is not any kind of
noise, but the kind of noise that exploits

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:36.520
biases in the neural network, which is, by
no means trivial to craft.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:42.750
So now, I hope you are expecting a sophisticated
adversarial attack against the wonderful CLIP

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:45.069
neural network.

00:05:45.069 --> 00:05:47.659
Yes, that will do.

00:05:47.659 --> 00:05:48.750
Or will it?

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.009
Let’s see together!

00:05:50.009 --> 00:05:58.080
Yes, indeed, I don’t know if you knew, but
this is not an apple, this is a pizza.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:05:59.889
And so is this one.

00:05:59.889 --> 00:06:05.530
The neural network fell for these ones, but
it was able to resist this sophisticated attack

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:08.919
in the case of the coffee mug and the phone.

00:06:08.919 --> 00:06:15.719
Perhaps the pizza labels had too small a footprint
in an image, so let’s try an even more sophisticated

00:06:15.719 --> 00:06:17.560
version of this attack.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:23.539
Now, you may think that this is a chihuahua,
but that is completely wrong, because this,

00:06:23.539 --> 00:06:25.770
is a pizza indeed.

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:29.569
Not a chihuahua in sight anywhere in this
image.

00:06:29.569 --> 00:06:30.840
No-no!

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.840
So what did we learn here?

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:38.430
Well, interestingly, this CLIP neural network
is more general than previous techniques,

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:41.990
however, its superpowers come at a price.

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:48.340
And that price is that it can be exploited
easily with simple systematic attacks.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:51.240
That is a great lesson indeed.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:54.759
Experiment number three, understanding feelings.

00:06:54.759 --> 00:06:57.120
Now this will be one heck of an experiment.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.849
We will try to answer the age-old question,
which is, how would you describe feelings

00:07:02.849 --> 00:07:03.849
to a machine?

00:07:03.849 --> 00:07:09.680
Well, it’s hard to explain such a concept,
but all humans understand what being bored

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:10.680
means.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:15.900
However, luckily, these neural networks have
neurons, and they can use those to explain

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:19.479
to us what they think about different concepts.

00:07:19.479 --> 00:07:25.130
An interesting idea here is that feelings
could sort of emerge as a combination of other,

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:28.930
more elementary neurons that are already understood.

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:33.900
If this sounds a little nebulous, let’s
go with that example, what does the machine

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:37.039
think it means that someone is bored?

00:07:37.039 --> 00:07:41.939
Well, it says that bored, is relaxed + grumpy.

00:07:41.939 --> 00:07:46.960
This isn’t quite the way I think about it,
but not bad at all, little machine.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:48.530
I like the way you think.

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:50.580
Let’s try one more.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:54.139
How do we explain to a machine what a surprise
means?

00:07:54.139 --> 00:07:59.159
Well, it says surprise is celebration + shock.

00:07:59.159 --> 00:08:00.159
Nice.

00:08:00.159 --> 00:08:02.340
What about madness?

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:03.780
Let’s see.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:08.580
Evil + serious + a hint of mental illness.

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:13.370
And when talking about combinations, there
are two more examples that I really liked.

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:18.900
If we are looking for text that embodies evil,
we get something like this.

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:21.430
And now, give me an evil building.

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:27.590
Oh yes, I think that works really well, but
there are internet forums where we have black-belt

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:32.620
experts specializing in this very topic, so,
if you are one of them, please let me know

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:34.620
in the comments what you think.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:40.020
The paper also contains a ton more information,
for instance, there is an experiment with

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:41.430
the Stroop effect.

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:48.050
This explores whether the neural network reacts
to the meaning of the text, or the color of

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:49.050
the text?

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:53.380
I will only tease this because I would really
like you to read the paper, which is available

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:55.960
below in the video description.

00:08:55.960 --> 00:09:01.680
So there we go, neural networks are by no
means brains in a jar, they are very much

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:07.890
computer programs, however, CLIP has some
similarities, and we also found that there

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:09.970
is a cost to that.

00:09:09.970 --> 00:10:07.760
What a time to be alive!

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.190
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

